---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CaseHead from '@/components/CaseHead.astro';
import Diverging from '@/components/charts/Diverging.astro';
import Bars from '@/components/charts/Bars.astro';
import DotInterval from '@/components/charts/DotInterval.astro';
import { repos } from '@/data/site';

// All figures below are transcribed from the published write-up (README, commit 0415eda, 2026-09-24).
const holds = [
  { hold: '10 s', trades: 448, hit: '50%', gross: '+0.73', tg: '+2.09', spread: '2.14', fees: '3.82', net: '−5.22', tn: '−14.91' },
  { hold: '30 s', trades: 201, hit: '49%', gross: '+1.38', tg: '+1.69', spread: '2.28', fees: '3.82', net: '−4.72', tn: '−5.80' },
  { hold: '60 s', trades: 131, hit: '52%', gross: '+2.35', tg: '+1.96', spread: '2.26', fees: '3.81', net: '−3.72', tn: '−3.10', best: true },
  { hold: '120 s', trades: 87, hit: '43%', gross: '+1.72', tg: '+0.71', spread: '2.06', fees: '3.81', net: '−4.15', tn: '−1.72' },
  { hold: '300 s', trades: 53, hit: '32%', gross: '−4.71', tg: '−1.11', spread: '2.22', fees: '3.81', net: '−10.73', tn: '−2.53' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="VN30 index futures: order flow and the turnover-tax floor · Tung Tran"
  description="A self-recorded tick dataset for Vietnam's index futures, a trade-flow imbalance signal tested out of sample, and a +2.35-tick gross edge that fees, tax and spread turn into −3.72 ticks per trade (t = −3.10)."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <CaseHead
      title="VN30 index futures: an order‑flow edge that costs erase"
      lede="Does exchange-labelled order flow predict Vietnam’s index futures over seconds to minutes? And does any edge survive a tax levied on turnover rather than profit? The signal is there. It is smaller than the toll."
      meta={[
        ['Period', 'Aug 2026 – now'],
        ['Type', 'Personal research'],
        ['Market', 'VN30F front month, HNX'],
        ['Stack', 'Python, pandas, PyArrow/Parquet, pytest'],
      ]}
      links={[
        { label: 'Code and write-up ↗', href: repos.vn30, primary: true },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="doc">
      <h2>Why the cost side is the point</h2>
      <p>
        VN30 futures are the one liquid instrument in Vietnam that can express a short-horizon view in either
        direction: equities cannot be sold short and settle T+2. But personal income tax on derivatives is
        charged on <strong>turnover, not profit</strong>. Every side of every trade pays it, win or lose. And
        the margin rate sits inside the tax base, so a regulator’s margin change rewrites the cost function.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="spec">
          <caption>
            Table 1. Round-trip cost for one contract at 1,884 index points, before spread. The tax formula
            reproduces the regulator’s worked example to the exact đồng.
          </caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Margin regime</th><th class="n">Fees (VND)</th><th class="n">Tax (VND)</th><th class="n">Total (VND)</th><th class="n">Break-even</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>10% (to Jul 2018)</td><td class="n">5,400</td><td class="n">18,840</td><td class="n">24,240</td><td class="n">2.42 ticks</td></tr>
            <tr><td>13% (Jul 2018 – Dec 2022)</td><td class="n">5,400</td><td class="n">24,492</td><td class="n">29,892</td><td class="n">2.99 ticks</td></tr>
            <tr class="is-best"><td>17% (since 15 Dec 2022)</td><td class="n">5,400</td><td class="n">32,028</td><td class="n">37,428</td><td class="n">3.74 ticks</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Because the tax scales with the index while the tick value is fixed, the break-even drifts with the
        market: 2.89 to 4.14 ticks over the sample, median 3.81. Add the median two-tick spread and a taker pays
        about six ticks per round trip. The fiscal part is the larger one.
      </p>

      <h2>Building a dataset that doesn’t exist</h2>
      <p>
        There is no public tick dataset for VN30 futures and none purchasable from outside Vietnam. The broker
        feed I planned to use advertises anonymous market-data topics but refuses every anonymous connection, so
        the recorder calls another broker’s public HTTP endpoints directly. Those carry the exchange’s own
        aggressor flag on each trade (no Lee–Ready classifier needed) and board snapshots with three depth
        levels, open interest and foreign flow, roughly every two seconds.
      </p>
      <p>
        Trades and snapshots disappear at the close; a session not recorded is gone. So capture started before
        any modelling: <strong>320k+ trades across eight sessions</strong>, 1-minute bars for 327 trading days,
        and daily bars back to 2018 with <strong>97 contract rolls</strong> back-adjusted. The package is about
        2.6k lines of Python with 43 pytest tests, each pinning a fact that can be checked outside the code: the
        regulator’s tax example, the third-Thursday expiry rule, the roll alignment, the trade identity, the
        absence of look-ahead.
      </p>

      <h2>The check that caught the bugs</h2>
      <p>
        Each trade carries the exchange’s running volume total. If the recorder holds every trade of a session,
        the recorded sizes must sum to the largest running total seen; any shortfall is exactly the volume missing,
        any excess is double counting. That turns “is the data complete?” into a number that has to be zero before
        a session enters the study.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The timestamp was not a trade identity.</strong> Fetching the same page twice returned the same
          trade with a different sub-second stamp. De-duplicating on timestamp, price and size let repeats in and
          inflated one session to 1.5× the exchange’s volume: 310,612 recorded against a counter of 200,484.
          Trades are now keyed on the running total.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A network error read as the end of the data.</strong> After the laptop woke from sleep the first
          request failed, the sweep treated the exception as “no more pages”, and the log reported zero missing
          trades for a session missing most of its morning. Only an empty page ends a sweep now.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The roll adjustment was one bar early.</strong> After the fix, the adjusted move across each roll
          equals the incoming contract’s own return to within 2.3 × 10<sup>−13</sup> points.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        None of these raised an error. Four of the eight recorded sessions pass the identity and a book check;
        those four (9,244 book states, 6.3 hours of continuous trading) are the study sample. Bars agree with a
        second provider on 99.3% of 32,917 overlapping minutes.
      </p>

      <h2>How long you must hold to clear the toll</h2>
      <Bars
        label="Median absolute move of the front month by horizon, in ticks, against the fees-and-tax floor"
        digits={1}
        unit=" ticks"
        max={36}
        mark={{ value: 3.81, label: 'Fees + tax, median 3.81 ticks (spread excluded)' }}
        rows={[
          { label: '1 minute', value: 6.0 },
          { label: '5 minutes', value: 13.0 },
          { label: '15 minutes', value: 23.0 },
          { label: '30 minutes', value: 33.0 },
        ]}
      >
        <b>Figure 1.</b> Median absolute move of the front month over h minutes, May 2025 to September 2026
        (77,561 one-minute windows at h = 1). The floor is 64% of a typical one-minute move and 12% of a
        30-minute one. Closing prices carry bid–ask bounce, so this comparison flatters intraday trading.
      </Bars>
      <p>
        The tax doesn’t rule out intraday trading; it pushes the viable holding period out toward tens of
        minutes. Order-flow predictability is known to decay within minutes. Whether those two windows overlap is
        the question the rest of the study answers.
      </p>

      <h2>What the order flow predicts</h2>
      <p>
        The signal is trade-flow imbalance: buyer-initiated minus seller-initiated contracts over the trailing 30
        seconds, counting only trades stamped at least a second before each snapshot. Returns are mid to mid, so a
        signal is never scored against the bid–ask bounce it helped create. Contemporaneously, flow and price move
        together (correlation +0.815). That is a sanity check that the recorded trades and the recorded book describe the
        same market.
      </p>
      <DotInterval
        label="Correlation of trade-flow imbalance with the forward mid-price move, by horizon, with 95% block-bootstrap intervals"
        min={-0.15}
        max={0.25}
        ticks={[-0.1, 0, 0.1, 0.2]}
        rows={[
          { label: '10 s', value: 0.07, lo: -0.015, hi: 0.134 },
          { label: '30 s', value: 0.118, lo: -0.007, hi: 0.205 },
          { label: '60 s', value: 0.129, lo: -0.021, hi: 0.229, highlight: true },
          { label: '120 s', value: 0.051, lo: -0.101, hi: 0.151 },
          { label: '300 s', value: 0.01, lo: -0.104, hi: 0.101 },
        ]}
      >
        <b>Figure 2.</b> Predictive correlation peaks between 30 and 60 seconds and is gone by five minutes.
        That is the profile the microstructure literature predicts. It is positive in all four sessions at the peak, but
        every 95% interval (block bootstrap, 5-minute blocks) includes zero. Four sessions are evidence of a small
        effect, not proof of one.
      </DotInterval>

      <h2>The out-of-sample trading rule</h2>
      <p>
        When |imbalance| exceeds its 80th percentile (a threshold computed on the <em>other</em> sessions only, so
        it never sees the session it trades), take one contract in the direction of the flow, crossing the spread
        on entry and exit, and hold for a fixed time. Costs come from the cost model at the actual fill prices.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="spec">
          <caption>Table 2. Ticks per trade, leave-one-session-out. Fills at the quoted touch with no queue, impact or latency. Every omission favours the strategy.</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Hold</th><th class="n">Trades</th><th class="n">Hit</th><th class="n">Gross</th><th class="n">t</th>
              <th class="n">Spread</th><th class="n">Fees + tax</th><th class="n">Net</th><th class="n">t</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {holds.map((h) => (
              <tr class:list={{ 'is-best': h.best }}>
                <td>{h.hold}</td><td class="n">{h.trades}</td><td class="n">{h.hit}</td><td class="n">{h.gross}</td>
                <td class="n">{h.tg}</td><td class="n">{h.spread}</td><td class="n">{h.fees}</td>
                <td class="n">{h.net}</td><td class="n">{h.tn}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <Diverging
        label="Ticks per trade at a 60-second hold"
        min={-4.5}
        max={3}
        rows={[
          { label: 'Gross edge of the signal', value: 2.35, text: '+2.35', kind: 'pos' },
          { label: 'Spread paid as a taker', value: -2.26, text: '−2.26', kind: 'neg' },
          { label: 'Exchange fee + turnover tax', value: -3.81, text: '−3.81', kind: 'neg' },
          { label: 'Net per trade', value: -3.72, text: '−3.72', kind: 'net' },
        ]}
      >
        <b>Figure 3.</b> The best hold, decomposed. Hit rates sit near 50%: the gross edge comes from larger moves
        in the signalled direction, not from being right more often.
      </Diverging>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          The best gross edge, +2.35 ticks, is smaller than fees and tax alone. A maker paying no spread would still
          lose about 1.5 ticks a trade; a taker loses 3.72 (t = −3.10). To break even the signal would have to be
          about 2.6 times stronger. The signal lives at 30–60 seconds; the tax needs a holding period over which it
          has already decayed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>What this doesn’t show</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Sample size.</strong> Four sessions and 6.3 hours of book. The predictive correlations are not
          significant on their own; the negative net result is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Book cadence.</strong> Two-second snapshots rule out event-level order-flow imbalance, so the
          exact aggressor-signed trade flow stands in for it. A faster feed could sharpen the signal; it would not
          change the cost of trading on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One live source.</strong> Bars are cross-checked against a second provider; ticks cannot be.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Capture.</strong> A workstation that sleeps is not a recording host. It lost more sessions than
          the code did. An always-on machine is the precondition for a larger sample.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The data can’t be redistributed, so the repository ships the recorder, the curation steps, the quality gate
        and the two study scripts rather than the dataset. <a href={repos.vn30} rel="noopener">Reproduction steps are
        in the README.</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
